    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Immunodeficient “nude” rats are widely used for spinal cord injury research despite the lack of neuroanatomy characterization specific to this rat. This research has continued without characterization of the neuroanatomy because, from a neurological perspective, nude rats are assumed to be faithful models for non-immunodeficient rats. This assumption is made despite the neuroendocrine differences associated with nude rats being athymic, as well as other, more subtle differences that have been observed in the spinal column of nude rats. In this study we characterized the motor neuron pools which innervate the forelimb muscles of the RNU-/- nude rat and also investigated the extent to which the motor neuron pools differed in number and localization between RNU-/- nude rats and RNU+/- non-nude rats as a control. Using the tracer Fluoro-Gold, we performed retrograde tracing of four different forelimb muscles as well as behavioral testing. We ultimately found a 16-fold reduction in the number of labeled motor neurons innervating the forelimb muscles of nude rats in comparison to non-nude rats. We were also able to localize the forelimb motor neuron pools of the RNU rats for the first time, which appeared to span a longer range of vertebrae than has previously been described in other rats. These findings of neuroanatomical differences in the RNU-/- rat serve to demonstrate confounding variables that are not currently being accounted for in spinal cord injury research. Further, our characterization data supports the notion that the nude rat may have important differences to the non-nude rat.",
